1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,568
Describe rhythm along two axes: where the impulse originates and how
it conducts. A sinus origin does not guarantee normal AV conduction,
and a narrow QRS does not identify the atrial pacemaker. Use the six
steps to establish each feature instead of jumping from one visual
clue to a label.

2
00:00:20,568 --> 00:00:38,880
For the reference sinus pattern, look for a consistent upright P
before each QRS in lead two, one-to-one conduction, a regular rate in
the adult teaching range and appropriate intervals. This is a
reference pattern, not proof that the patient has no cardiac disease.

3
00:00:38,880 --> 00:00:59,640
Sinus bradycardia preserves the sinus relationships but has a rate
below sixty. Causes include conditioning, vagal effects, hypothermia,
hypoxia, nodal ischemia and medication effects. Assess symptoms and
perfusion; the rhythm-classification cutoff is not automatically a
treatment trigger.

4
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:21,768
Sinus tachycardia is above one hundred with the same sinus pattern.
Look for pain, fever, hypovolemia, hypoxia, anxiety, pulmonary illness
or stimulants. Reducing a compensatory rate without correcting the
cause can worsen output. A fast number alone does not establish re-
entry SVT.

5
00:01:21,768 --> 00:01:41,256
Respiratory sinus arrhythmia produces cyclic rate variation, commonly
in younger people, while the P-to-QRS relationship remains sinus.
Confirm the pattern rather than calling every irregular rhythm AF. Not
all irregularity has the same mechanism or urgency.

6
00:01:41,256 --> 00:02:01,896
A sinus pause or arrest presents a missing expected atrial impulse,
sometimes followed by an escape beat. Review vagal influences,
medication and sinus-node disease. Recurrent or symptomatic pauses
merit urgent evaluation; a short strip may not establish the exact
pause mechanism.

7
00:02:01,896 --> 00:02:21,000
A premature atrial contraction starts early from an atrial focus
outside the sinus node. Look for an early P with a different shape; it
may be partly hidden in the preceding T wave. The following QRS is
often narrow but may be aberrant or absent if conduction fails.

8
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:42,888
Assess frequency, change from baseline and the patient rather than
treating one isolated PAC as an emergency. Stress, caffeine,
electrolyte disturbance and structural disease are possible
associations. Frequent atrial ectopy may justify closer observation
and follow-up without proving AF on that strip.

9
00:02:42,888 --> 00:03:04,392
Wandering atrial pacemaker shows changing P-wave shapes as the
dominant atrial focus shifts. At least three distinct P morphologies
support the school pattern. When that multifocal atrial pattern is
tachycardic, above one hundred per minute, it is called multifocal
atrial tachycardia rather than simple wandering pacemaker.

10
00:03:04,392 --> 00:03:26,424
MAT can be associated with significant pulmonary disease and metabolic
stress. Unlike AF, discernible P waves with multiple shapes are
present. Compare multiple beats, assess oxygenation and physiology,
and communicate the rhythm and underlying illness rather than assuming
one universal rate-control solution.

11
00:03:26,424 --> 00:03:45,984
Atrial flutter is organized rapid atrial activity, often with a
sawtooth appearance in inferior leads. The school reference is an
atrial rate around two hundred fifty to three hundred fifty. Count
atrial and ventricular activity separately because not every atrial
impulse conducts.

12
00:03:45,984 --> 00:04:07,920
With fixed conduction, the ventricular rhythm may be very regular;
with changing conduction, it may vary. Flutter waves can hide in QRS
or T waves. Structural and valvular disease or pulmonary illness may
contribute. Urgency depends on perfusion and symptoms, not on seeing a
sawtooth alone.

13
00:04:07,920 --> 00:04:27,624
Atrial fibrillation combines disorganized atrial activity with an
irregularly irregular ventricular response when AV conduction is
variable. Look for the absence of consistent discrete P waves across a
sufficient sample. Noise or a single irregular interval is not enough
evidence.

14
00:04:27,624 --> 00:04:49,752
AF can reduce coordinated atrial contribution and may carry
thromboembolic risk. Obtain onset and medication history when
feasible. If the patient is unstable, seek urgent authorized care; the
school shorthand about rate control must not be read as permission for
a PCP to choose antiarrhythmic treatment independently.

15
00:04:49,752 --> 00:05:09,048
The school describes PSVT as typically narrow, regular and very rapid
with abrupt onset and offset, often without easily visible P waves.
The rate may exceed one hundred fifty, but that number alone does not
distinguish SVT from sinus tachycardia or flutter.

16
00:05:09,048 --> 00:05:30,336
Ask how it started, inspect atrial activity and look for a cause of
compensatory tachycardia. Vagal manoeuvres and medicines require the
applicable service pathway and authority; do not turn a recognition
lecture into a treatment order. Instability requires urgent
escalation, not prolonged naming exercises.

17
00:05:30,336 --> 00:05:46,392
Organize causes into autonomic, structural, metabolic and medication-
related groups. This helps connect the history to the tracing and
prevents a rhythm label from ending assessment. The same named rhythm
can have different causes in two patients.

18
00:05:46,392 --> 00:06:09,384
Check for altered mentation, shock signs, ischemic discomfort and
serious respiratory compromise. Decide whether the rhythm is causing
deterioration or is a response to another problem. Recognition here
supports monitoring, communication and escalation; detailed treatment
decisions are the later ECG 201 scope.
